</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">An analysis of Beethoven’s ‘senza sordino’ marking in the first movement of his Piano Sonata in C sharp minor op. 27 no. 2, including the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>     views of his pupils Carl Czerny and Anton Schindler.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> Wensleydale Press, Sydney 2009. ISBN 978-0-9807452-1-4.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[5] L. v. Beethoven; B. Bloch (editor, fingering); P. Hauschild, J. Reutter (editors): 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">Klaviersonate op. 27/2 Mondscheinsonate.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> Wiener Urtext Edition, Vienna
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>     2019. ISMN 979-0-50057-416-3, UT 50433.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="9">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="1588.15" justify="left" valign="top">the stems of G♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>4
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>. This edition reproduces the markings above the staff for legibility since this does not impede
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>comprehension of the intended meaning.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m25: Treble and bass staves are not vertically aligned in A. This edition infers that Beethoven aligns “cres:”
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>with the notes in the bass staff (as “Cres.” also appears in C) and puts 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">cresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> at the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat. If it were to be
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>understood as aligned with the notes in the treble staff, the position of 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">cresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> would be between the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> and 3
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>rd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>half-beats in A. H puts it at the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat. Also cf. analogous m48, m58, where there’s no doubt about the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>intended position of 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">cresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">m27/28: A″ shows no dynamic markings besides 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin">. C provides the dynamics indicated by brackets and
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>superscript 1 in this edition. This is how most editions put it and how it’s usually performed. Nevertheless, in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the absence of dynamic markings in A″, this edition also includes the 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> marking in m27 of A′, indicated by
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>brackets and superscript 2. “Decres.” in C is aligned with the 6
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">, as this edition shows it. It may be intended
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>to apply at the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat instead, where H puts it.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m40, m66: A and C place 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">decresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> (“decres:”/“dcre:” and “Decres.” respectively) in the bass staff in these cases
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>where three voices share the staff and the treble staff remains empty. This edition reproduces this accurately
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>since the author’s apparent intent is to ensure that they’re not misunderstood as only applying to the upper
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>voice(s).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m60: The 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> marking in A appears aligned with G♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> in the LH (
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">left hand
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">) up voice, even though ever so
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>slightly too low to say with certainty. C and H place it below the bass staff. This edition follows Chapman’s
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>argument
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[6]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> for the author’s intent in this instance and aligns 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-weight="bold" font-style="italic" font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-weight="normal" font-style="normal" font-family="Edwin"> with the LH up voice. (See also Stem
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Direction below in further support.)
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m62/63: C places the 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> between the staves, A above the treble staff. There’s also the faint remnant of a
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>carefully erased 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> between the staves in m62 of A, leaving little doubt that the placement between the staves
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>is in error, and that the swell should be understood to apply only to the RH; as conversely in m64/65 only to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the LH. Therefore, this edition follows A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Accidentals and Pitch
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">A often abbreviates consecutive triplet groups when they are identical. C occasionally repeats the accidentals
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>on these groups (m14, m19, m20). Those are not included in this edition. Beethoven sometimes neglects to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>notate necessary accidentals after the barline (also m11?). C corrects this for m21. Accidentals not found in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>either A or C (but in H) and that should be present without doubt are given in brackets (m11, m15).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>This edition includes all evidently intentional courtesy accidentals even when this is overly cautious under
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>modern conventions: m4 (but see below), m12, m17, m19, m20, m26, m35 (but see below). C does not include
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the courtesy ♯ on D♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>5
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> in m26 as it directly follows the key signature there.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m4: The redundant ♯s on the 11
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>, 12
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">s in C are omitted by this edition.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m20: A and C have a D♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> (with accidental) in the LH. This is widely accepted to be in error for E♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>, as H and
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>this edition put it. A, C, H, and this edition agree on pitch in every other case.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m35: C includes a courtesy ♮♯ that is not found in A. It appears to be a late correction to the engraved plate,
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>set across the barline as remaining space necessitated, suggesting Beethoven insisted on its inclusion. This
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>edition reproduces it after the barline and, in analogy to other courtesy accidentals, inserts it on the octave in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>brackets. A/C have a ♯ on D♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>5
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> but not on D♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>6
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>. This edition omits it as D♯ already lacks courtesy ♯s in m27-m32.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Stem Direction
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">It’s apparent that Beethoven instructed C to copy stem direction from A accurately: A and C are identical
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>except in the LH in m54 (A up, C down). Thus it can be surmised that stem direction is quite intentional and
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in any case conforms to common practices of the time.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="9">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="64.01" justify="left" valign="bottom" font-family="Arial" font-size="9">______________________________________________________________________________________________________
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin">References
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="5"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="7">[6] Chapman 2015: 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">Grief, Denial and Rage: A Revisionist View of Beethoven’s “Moonlight” Sonata.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> Proceedings of the 12
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> Australasian Piano Pedagogy
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>     Conference, Melbourne.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="10">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="1587.01" justify="left" valign="top">When there’s only one voice in the LH (m1-m59), it is up or down stemmed (per measure) as best suits the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>available space. No meaning is conveyed by this. This edition therefore follows C for m54 as it is better suited
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>for print and mirrors the analogous m52.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The triplet accompaniment voice is generally stemmed such as space allows, with attention given to stemming
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>neighboring similar groups the same. When this voice serves as the main melodic voice in the RH, in m32-
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m37 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat and from m61 on, Beethoven uses stem direction (in the treble staff) to indicate phrases.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>In m60, the dotted rhythm fragment of the first subject (m5-m9) reappears as the up voice in the LH. Stem
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>direction is carefully maintained in A and C, even though this necessitates incredibly short stems in m63/m65
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>4
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> half-beats. The beams in A even cross in m65; C employs curved beams. Only in m66 is G♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> down stemmed,
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>resulting in two down voices in the bass staff. The upper down voice in m66 is to be understood as distinct
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>from the up voice in m60-m65 which has concluded:
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-weight="bold" font-style="italic"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-weight="normal" font-style="normal" font-family="Edwin"> no longer applies.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Rests
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">m9, m23: There are no half rests in C or A. H inserts rests in parentheses. In the absence of A for m9, C has to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>be taken as it appears. Nevertheless, this edition follows H and includes a rest in brackets. This edition does
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>however not insert a rest in m23 as it may be the author’s intent for the RH melody and accompaniment
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>voices to be understood as merging in the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat of m23. Peculiar beaming of the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> triplet group might
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>suggest as much; cf. the corresponding groups in m5, m42, m60. The “missing” cadence in the RH has long
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>troubled editors such as Liszt (1857),
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[7]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> who first added a 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> stem to the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> of m23, which has become a
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>common practice since.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[8]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Slurs
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">Assessing the author’s actual intent is difficult. The slurs in A were often not drawn with great care, even
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>compared to the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> and 3
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>rd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> movements. It can be speculated that this is because Beethoven placed more
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>importance on comprehensive indication in the much faster tempi, and because legato vs. non-legato execution
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>is trivial given the “senza sordino” instruction (according to Czerny, the whole piece “must be played
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">legatissimo
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">”
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[9]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>). Nevertheless, this edition strives to reproduce the intended slurring as accurately as possible.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>C interprets the slurs in A rather poorly in places. From comparing C to A, it can be surmised that the slurs
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in m1-m13 are not an accurate representation of A, especially in m12, m13. However, any attempt at
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>interpretation runs the risk of deviating even further from the author's intent than C already likely does.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Thus, this edition reproduces the slurs in m1-m13 as found in C. But the principal source for the slurs from
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m14 on is always A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Beethoven sometimes draws slurs in a “parenthesis” style, enveloping notes rather than beginning and ending
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the slur on specific ones. This edition normalizes such slurs as no meaning is conveyed by this. He rarely
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>extends slurs over barlines, often drawing them to the end of a measure. This style is reproduced in this
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>edition as Beethoven predates modern phrasing conventions. He treats slurs before and over system breaks
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>inconsistently. This edition has to rely on inference, analogy, and C in cases where his intent is intransparent.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>H’s interpretation is referenced when the intended slurring is doubtful. Uncertainties are as follows.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m25: The lower slur in A extends to the chord in m26. The upper slur terminates at the end of the measure.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>However, it seems to curve down early since F♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> in m23 above is in the way; the slur even touches the note. It
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>might be in the author’s actual intent for the upper slur to extend to D♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>4
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> in m26, mirroring the lower slur. On
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the other hand, the analogous slurs in m48, m58 terminate at the end of their measures, suggesting the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>extended lower slur might be the unintentional anomaly. C does not extend the lower slur but places it above
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the bass staff where it crosses through “Cres.”, suggesting the inclusion of one or the other is a subsequent
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>correction. Beethoven’s intent being this opaque, this edition refrains from interpretation and reproduces the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>slurs as they appear in A, as H does.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m27: The lower slur in A″ appears in a parenthesis style around the </credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14"></credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> octaves in the 2</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat. C and H have it</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="10">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="64.01" justify="left" valign="bottom" font-family="Arial" font-size="9">______________________________________________________________________________________________________
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin">References
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="5"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="7">[7] L. v. Beethoven; Franz Liszt (editor): 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">Sonaten für das Pianoforte Solo von Ludwig van Beethoven.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> Ludwig Holle, Wolfenbüttel 1857.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[8] King Yue Li 2020: 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">Nineteenth-century Performance and Editorial Practice: A Study of Beethoven’s Sonata in C-sharp minor, Op. 27 No. 2.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> Arizona State
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>     University.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[9] Carl Czerny: </credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">On the Proper Performance of All Beethoven’s Works for the Piano.</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> R. Cocks &amp; Co., London 1846.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="11">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="1587.01" justify="left" valign="top">from one to the other. However, the placement of the slur over m30 in the next system of A″ leaves too little
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>space to have begun the slur under m27 on the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat as in the analogous m25, m48, m58. The slurs in m27
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>are in a thinner pen than the slur over m30, suggesting they were done at different times and not necessarily
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in consecutive order. The lower slur of m27 in A′, having sufficient space there, does indeed begin on the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>beat. A″ and A′ are otherwise identical in m27-m31 (excluding dynamics; see there). Therefore, this edition
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>follows A′ for the slurs in m27.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m30: C has a slur over the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> triplet group to the 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> up stem on the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> half-beat that is not present in either
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>version of m30 in A. This edition includes it as found in C since it seems unlikely to be an interpolation not
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>sanctioned by the author. This is however uncertain as m28, m29, m31 lack corresponding slurs. Though this
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>might be explained by their 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> half-beats not being strictly analogous to m30. H omits the slur.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m35/36: The slur over m35 as initially drawn in A″ terminates at the end of the measure. However, Beethoven
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>corrected this at a later time by drawing a straighter end to the slur in thicker ink, extending it slightly over
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the barline but not quite connecting it to the slur of m36. C has a system break at this position and does not
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>indicate the slurs to be connected. H indicates this particularity by ending the slur of m35 and starting the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>slur of m36 both on the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> of m36. A′ lacks slurs in m32-m36 altogether. This edition infers that the intent
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>of the alteration in A″ is for the slurs to be connected, as stem direction also suggests a continuous phrase,
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>and shows it as such.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m37: A″ and C end the slur over the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat on the 6
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">. A″ appears to begin the slur under the RH down
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>voice in the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat on the down stem 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> and to end it slightly after the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> (the augmented) 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">. However, as
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>mentioned, Beethoven often draws slurs in a parenthesis style, making it unclear where a slur is intended to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>begin or end exactly. A″ also has a system (and page) break after m37, which Beethoven treats inconsistently.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>C does not include this lower slur. H’s interpretation shifts these slurs one 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> to the right (that is, their end
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>and start respectively) and terminates the second on the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>This edition ends the slur over the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat as it appears in A″ and C, on the last 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> of the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat. For the start 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>of the lower slur, H’s interpretation is followed. For its end, this edition extends it to the end of the measure.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>While it seems to terminate in A″ as H puts it (albeit in a parenthesis style), this has to be viewed in the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>context of the page break. Taken into consideration is also the crossed out m37 of A′, with the corresponding
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>lower slur indeed drawn to the end of the measure in the absence of a page (or system) break. Although this
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>measure differs from m37 in A″ in a number of ways (cf. Appendix).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The slur over the RH up voice in the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat is drawn in a different pen in A″, suggesting it was done at a
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>different time. It is unclear whether the presence of both slurs (on the up and down voices) is in the author’s
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>intent or the one is supposed to be superseding the other, and in that case which one which. The absence of
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the lower slur in C might suggest the latter. A′ lacks the upper slur. This edition reproduces the slur as found
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in A″ and C, as H does.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m39: The slur under m39, in contrast to the analogous slurs in m37/38, crosses into m40 in A. The only one of
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>these measures not before a system break in A is m39 (except m37 in A′). In m40, there’s a 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> down stem on the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>first 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> that is crossed out and replaced by the present down stem 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">. The slur does not cross the barline in C. It
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>is therefore quite uncertain whether this slur crosses the barline because of inconsistent drawing, a previously
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>intended difference that was not corrected in A (but in C), or an actually intended difference in the final
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>instance (that C failed to include). Given this uncertainty, this edition refrains from interpretation, follows H,
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>and reproduces the slur as found in A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m55/56 (m7): A exhibits many crossed out notes in m56. The slurs appear in Beethoven’s style of ties. It is
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>unclear whether they’re in the author’s intent or a leftover of a previously intended tied octave. C, following
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Beethoven’s different styles for ties and slurs, also gives them in the form of ties. H puts them as slurs without
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>further comment. They’re indicated by dashed lines in this edition. Something similar might be the case in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m7. It is impossible to say in the absence of A. Although m7 is otherwise identical to m44, which shows a
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>single lower slur in A and C. H omits the upper LH slur of m7 in analogy to m44, but this edition reproduces
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the slurs as found in C and refrains from indicating this uncertainty beyond this comment.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m62-m65: A has a system break at m62/63 and slurs over each measure. That the slurs are connected over the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>break is not apparent from A. C does not have a slur over m62 and shows a continuous slur over m64/65, both
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>contrary to A. H follows C but adds the missing (discrete) slur over m62 and ends the slur over m63 at the up
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>stemmed 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">, as it appears to in A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Even though C does not accurately reproduce the slurs in A, this is mostly limited to imprecision of their start</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="12">
    <credit-words default-x="85.73" default-y="1587.01" justify="left" valign="top">and end points, and the occasional, presumably negligent, omission (m37, m48, m62). At no other place does C
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>connect slurs that are discrete in A, including the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> and 3
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>rd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> movements. Thus it must be speculated that the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>continuous slur over m64/65 in C is a result of Beethoven having given instructions to the C editor to connect
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the slurs, but also that he might have been vague and actually meant the slurs over m62/63, them being over
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>a break in A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Therefore, and on the basis of stem direction, the presence (and respectively absence) of the swell, and in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>analogy to m32-m37, this edition infers a continuous phrase in m62/63 and connects the slurs, and keeps the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>slurs over m64/65 discrete, as they clearly are in A. The slur over m62/63 ends on the last 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> of m63, as it
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>appears in C and in analogy to m65 despite its appearance in A.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m66/67: The slurs in A seem to be drawn hastily, each one being markedly offset to the right; the slur over the
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> beat of m66 so far that it appears to begin on the 2
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>nd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">, as H puts it. This edition does not consider this to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>be intentional and begins the slur on the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10">, as C puts it. Instead of the tie in the lowest voice, C shows a
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>slur from m66’s C♯
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>3
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> to m67. This is widely regarded to be a misinterpretation of the ambiguously drawn tie in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>A, as H and this edition put it.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words></credit-words>
    </credit>
